<Project>
	<!--
	Copy this file to Secrets.local.props (same folder) and fill in the real value.
	Secrets.local.props is gitignored — it never gets committed. This file (the
	.example template) is the only one tracked in git.

	FirebaseWebApiKeyEncoded supplies the Firebase Web API key for iOS/MacCatalyst/
	Android builds, base64-encoded (see FirebaseOptions.cs for why it's base64 and
	why that's obfuscation, not real secrecy). It is baked into the compiled assembly
	at build time via <AssemblyMetadata>, which works on real iOS/MacCatalyst/Android
	devices where a runtime environment variable would not (an Xcode/simulator launch
	doesn't inherit your shell's env, and an installed Android APK runs sandboxed with
	no access to the host machine's env even when installed via `adb install` from
	that same machine). Windows continues to read MY_APP_SECRET from the environment
	at runtime instead, since a Windows build here runs directly on the dev machine;
	this file has no effect on that platform.

	To (re)generate the encoded value from the raw key:
		echo -n "<raw-key>" | base64

	The AndroidSigning* properties sign Android builds with the app's own keystore. The
	keystore file (*.keystore) is gitignored as well; keep it next to this file, or give an
	absolute path. Leave AndroidSigningKeyStore empty to sign with the SDK's debug key
	instead. CI gets the same values from repository secrets (.github/workflows/build.yml).
	-->
	<PropertyGroup>
		<FirebaseWebApiKeyEncoded></FirebaseWebApiKeyEncoded>
		<AndroidSigningKeyStore></AndroidSigningKeyStore>
		<AndroidSigningKeyAlias></AndroidSigningKeyAlias>
		<AndroidSigningStorePass></AndroidSigningStorePass>
		<AndroidSigningKeyPass></AndroidSigningKeyPass>
	</PropertyGroup>
</Project>
